    We may therefore assume that $|\{v_1,v_2,v_3\}|= 3$. Choose any two vertices $u_2 \in v_2$, $u_3 \in v_3$ in $F$ satisfying $F(\{u_2,u_3\}) = E(\{v_2,v_3\}) = c_\triangle$. Note that such an edge exists by definition of $E_F$. Since $v_1 \in \mathcal{X}_F$ by assumption, we know that $|v_1| \ge 4$ and we can by the pigeonhole principle pick two distinct vertices $w_1,w_2 \in v_1$ by \cref{lem:Claim_2} satisfying $F(\{u_2, w_1\}) = F(\{u_2, w_2\}) = F(\{u_3, w_1\}) = F(\{u_3, w_2\}) = F(\{u_2,u_3\}) = c_\triangle$.  We know that $F(\{w_1,w_2\}) = E_F(\{v_1\}) \neq c_\triangle$ by construction as well as \cref{lem:Claim_2}. With these however, we get the contradiction that $F[\{u_2,u_3,w_1,w_2\}]$ is in $\mathcal{H}(2,1,0)$.
